<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Munda people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Munda are an Adivasi people of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, who speak Mundari, a language of the Austroasiatic family. Traditionally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers organised in villages under hereditary headmen, they resisted landlords and colonial rule, notably in the rising led by Birsa Munda in 1899–1900.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/munda-people.jpg" alt="Munda people" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Biplob Rahaman · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Munda_Men,_Dinajpur,_2010_by_Biplob_Rahman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
